    {'para': '**436.** The root 1⟨dā⟩ is inflected in precisely the same way, but with change everywhere of ⟨dh⟩ to ⟨d⟩, except where ⟨dh⟩ belongs to the ending.'},
    {'para': '**437.** The root 1⟨hā⟩, ‘quit, abandon’ (act.), drops the ⟨ā⟩ in weak forms where the ending begins with a vowel, and in the opt.; thus, indic. 3rd sing. ⟨jahāti⟩, pl. ⟨jahati⟩; impf. 3rd. sing. ⟨ajahāt⟩, pl. ⟨ajahus⟩; opt. ⟨jahyāt⟩. The 2nd sing. imv. is ⟨jahīhi⟩ or ⟨jahihi⟩. In the other weak forms before consonant-endings the stem is either ⟨jahī⟩ or ⟨jahi⟩; thus, ⟨jahīmas⟩ or ⟨jahimas⟩.'},
    {'para': '**438.** 1⟨mā⟩, ‘measure’ (mid.), and 2⟨hā⟩, ‘move, go’ (mid.), form'},
    {'para': '⟨mimī⟩ and ⟨jihī⟩ before consonant-endings, ⟨mim⟩ and ⟨jih⟩ before vowel-endings; thus, 3rd persons indic. ⟨mimīte⟩, ⟨mimāte⟩, ⟨mimate⟩.'},
    {'para': '**439.** ⟨hu⟩ ‘pour, sacrifice’ (act. and mid.), makes the 2nd sing. imv. ⟨juhudhi⟩; 3rd persons impf. ⟨ajuhot⟩, ⟨ajuhutām⟩, ⟨ajuhavus⟩.'},
    {'para': '**440.** 1. ⟨bhī⟩, ‘fear’ (act.), may shorten its vowel in weak forms; thus, ⟨bibhīmas⟩ or ⟨bibhimas⟩, ⟨bibhīyāt⟩ or ⟨bibhiyāt⟩. — 2. ⟨hrī⟩, ‘be ashamed’ (act.), changes its weak stem ⟨jihrī⟩ to ⟨jihriy⟩ before vowel-endings; thus, indic. 3rd persons ⟨jihreti⟩, ⟨jihrītas⟩, ⟨jihriyati⟩.'},
    {'para': '∗ Only a small proportion of the roots of this class retain the accent on the root-syllable in the strong forms. In the great majority, the accent is on the reduplication, both in the strong forms and in those weak forms whose endings begin with vowels.'},
    {'para': '∗ In combination with ⟨t⟩ or ⟨th⟩ of an ending, the ⟨dh⟩ of ⟨dadh⟩ does not give ⟨ddh⟩, but follows the general rule of aspirate and of surd and sonant combination; and the lost aspiration is thrown back upon the initial of the root.'},
